@node Transcode Quick Start
@section Quick Start

@table @kbd
@item M-x cmacs-transcode-video
Open a video session.  In Dired this queues the marked files; elsewhere it
prompts for a file or directory.
@item M-x cmacs-transcode-audio
The same for an audio session.
@item M-x cmacs-transcode
Open the queue buffer without adding anything (add files with @kbd{a}).
@end table

In the resulting @code{*cmacs-transcode*} buffer, press @kbd{a} to add more
files, tune options with the single-key commands, then @kbd{RET} to start.
Outputs are written to @file{output/} under the current project root by
default.  With the shipped defaults, a video job reproduces the author's usual
@command{compress_video --no-hwaccel --crf 26} invocation (H.265 video, Vorbis
audio, Matroska container); an audio job produces a 320@dmn{kbps} MP3.

@node Transcode Queue Buffer
@section The Queue Buffer

The @code{*cmacs-transcode*} buffer (major mode @code{cmacs-transcode-mode},
derived from @code{special-mode}) is both the control panel and the live
status view.  Its header shows the resolved backend and the current options;
each queued file is one line showing a status glyph (@code{…} queued,
@code{▶} running, @code{✔} done, @code{✗} failed, @code{–} skipped), the file
name, and --- while running --- the latest ffmpeg progress line.  A timer
(@code{cmacs-transcode-poll-interval}, default 2@dmn{s}) redraws the buffer
while jobs run; it also redraws on every state change and self-cancels when
the pool drains.

@table @kbd
@item h j k l
Move left/down/up/right.  The mode map is an Evil overriding map, so these
motion keys work under Doom/Evil as well as in vanilla Emacs (the action
commands avoid @kbd{h}/@kbd{j}/@kbd{k}/@kbd{l}).
@item a
Add a media file or directory (prefix arg: recurse directories).  Directories
match only the current session kind's extensions (@pxref{Transcode Recursive}).
@item A
Add a directory recursively (current session kind only).
@item d
Remove the job on the current line.
@item K
Kill the running job on the current line (no retry).
@item @key{RET}
Start transcoding the queued jobs.
@item p
Toggle parallelism between sequential and the configured job count.
@item P
Set the number of concurrent jobs.
@item c
Set the video codec (or, in an audio session, the audio format/codec).
@item Q
Set the CRF (video) or the custom bitrate (audio).
@item f
Set the container/format.
@item H
Cycle the video hwaccel state: off @arrow{} auto @arrow{} force @arrow{}
vulkan @arrow{} off.
@item o
Set the output directory.
@item m
Toggle the process-missing filter (only encode files lacking an output).
@item x
Toggle the process-existing filter (only re-encode files that already have
an output).
@item R
Toggle recursive directory-mirroring: adding a directory recurses into it and
mirrors its subdirectory tree under the output directory (@pxref{Transcode
Recursive}).  Applies to directories added after toggling.
@item M
Switch the session between video and audio.
@item E
Cycle where ffmpeg runs: @code{auto} → @code{local} → @code{remote}
(@pxref{Transcode Backends}).
@item L
Show the @code{*cmacs-transcode-log*} buffer (full ffmpeg output).
@item g
Redraw the buffer.
@item q
Quit the queue buffer (@code{quit-window}).
@item ?
Echo the key hints.
@end table

Each session holds a job list plus an options plist that is @emph{seeded} from
the @code{defcustom} values (@pxref{Transcode Options}) when the session is
created; the single-key commands override those per session without mutating
the customs.  Under Evil/Doom the mode map is made an overriding map so the
single-key bindings win in every state.

@node Transcode Recursive
@section Recursive Directory-Mirroring

By default, adding a directory queues only the media files @emph{directly}
inside it.  Turn on recursive mode (@code{cmacs-transcode-recursive}, or the
@kbd{R} key; a prefix arg to @kbd{a}, or the @kbd{A} key, also recurse) and
adding a directory walks it recursively, @emph{mirroring each input's relative
subdirectory tree under the output directory}.  The @file{output/} tree then
holds only the newly transcoded files, in the same folder layout as the source
--- ideal for a show whose seasons live in separate folders, or a band whose
albums do:

@example
 input                          output/ (audio session, format = mp3)
 album_01/01-song.flac      ->  album_01/01-song.mp3
 album_02/01-song.flac      ->  album_02/01-song.mp3
@end example

Add the @emph{parent} directory (the one containing @file{album_01/},
@file{album_02/}, @dots{}) with recursion on to reproduce this.  The relative
subdirectory is taken with respect to the directory you add, so adding a single
album folder yields a flat @file{output/}.  Nested output directories are
created automatically, and for a container backend each job bind-mounts its own
(nested) output directory, so the mirroring works identically on host and
container backends.  Recursion is applied at @emph{add} time, so toggling
@kbd{R} affects directories added afterwards.

@subsection Match the session kind

Adding a directory (@kbd{a} or @kbd{A}) only queues files whose extension
matches the @emph{current session kind} --- video extensions in a video
session, audio extensions in an audio session.  A music library of @file{.flac}
therefore appears empty in a @emph{video} session: switch to audio first with
@kbd{M} (or open the queue with @kbd{M-x cmacs-transcode-audio}).  When an add
matches nothing, the echo area explains why --- e.g.@: @samp{no video files
added from @dots{} --- but 42 audio file(s) are here; press M to switch to
audio}, or @samp{@dots{} N are in subdirs; press R (or A) to recurse} when
recursion was off --- instead of a silent @samp{added 0}.

@node Transcode Backends
@section Backends: Container or Host

The backend is resolved at runtime by @code{cmacs-transcode--resolve}, honoured
in this order:

@enumerate
@item
the @env{CMACS_TRANSCODE_CONTAINER_RUNTIME} environment variable, if set;
@item
the @code{cmacs-transcode-backend} option (@code{auto}, @code{podman},
@code{docker}, or @code{host});
@item
availability: @code{auto} tries podman, then docker, then a host
@command{ffmpeg}; an explicit choice falls back to whatever is available.
@end enumerate

For a container backend the input directory is bind-mounted at @file{/input}
and the output directory at @file{/output} (or a single @file{/config} mount
when the two coincide); ffmpeg receives the in-container paths.  ffprobe (used
for colour-metadata and width probing) runs in the same image via
@code{--entrypoint ffprobe}.  When hardware acceleration is active the DRI
device is passed through (@code{--device}), with the Vulkan vendor environment
variable set as needed.  A host backend runs the binaries directly on the real
paths.  @code{(cmacs-transcode-supported-p)} is non-@code{nil} when any backend
resolves.

@subsection Remote execution (TRAMP / SSH)

When the input is opened over TRAMP (@file{/ssh:host:/path/}), cmacs-transcode can
run ffmpeg on that @emph{remote host's hardware} rather than streaming the media
locally.  @code{cmacs-transcode-execution} (header @code{exec:}, key @kbd{E})
selects: @code{auto} (default; follow the input's host), @code{remote} (force the
input's remote host), or @code{local} (force local, copying a remote input down
first).  Work runs through TRAMP-aware primitives (@code{process-file},
@code{make-process :file-handler}), so the container/binary, temp files, and
output all live on the execution host, which needs podman/docker or a host
@command{ffmpeg}.

Two caveats: (1) TRAMP resolves remote programs via @code{tramp-remote-path},
which often omits Homebrew and @file{/usr/sbin} --- add
@code{(add-to-list 'tramp-remote-path 'tramp-own-remote-path)} if the remote
ffmpeg/podman is not found; (2) GPU/hwaccel auto-detection reads local device
nodes, so a remote job encodes in software in this version.  Per-host overrides of
the backend/image/device use connection-local variables
(@code{connection-local-set-profile-variables}); the global @code{defcustom}
defaults apply everywhere else, so no per-host setup is required.

@node Transcode Options
@section Options

Every knob is a @code{defcustom} in the @code{cmacs-transcode} group, so you can
set your own defaults in your init file, e.g.:

@example
(setq cmacs-transcode-crf 26
      cmacs-transcode-hwaccel 'off
      cmacs-transcode-parallel-jobs 4)
@end example

@subsection General and backend

@vtable @code
@item cmacs-transcode-backend
Preferred backend: @code{auto} (default), @code{podman}, @code{docker}, or
@code{host}.
@item cmacs-transcode-container-image
Container image (default @code{docker.io/linuxserver/ffmpeg:latest}).
@item cmacs-transcode-output-dir
Output directory, or @code{nil} for @file{output/} under the project root.
@item cmacs-transcode-parallel-jobs
Concurrent ffmpeg processes (default @code{1}, i.e.@: sequential).
@item cmacs-transcode-poll-interval
Seconds between live redraws (default @code{2}; @code{nil} disables).
@item cmacs-transcode-overwrite
Pass @option{-y} to overwrite outputs (default @code{t}).
@item cmacs-transcode-max-retries
Attempts per video job before it fails (default @code{3}; audio is tried once).
@item cmacs-transcode-process-filter
@code{nil}, @code{missing}, or @code{existing}.
@item cmacs-transcode-hwaccel-device
VAAPI render node (default @file{/dev/dri/renderD128}).
@end vtable

@subsection Video

@vtable @code
@item cmacs-transcode-video-codec
Default codec key (default @code{"h265"}); see
@code{cmacs-transcode-video-codec-map}.
@item cmacs-transcode-video-audio-codec
Audio codec for video output (default @code{"vorbis"}).
@item cmacs-transcode-video-format
Container format (default @code{"mkv"}).
@item cmacs-transcode-quality
Named preset used when @code{cmacs-transcode-crf} is @code{nil} (default
@code{"medium"}).
@item cmacs-transcode-crf
CRF for software/VAAPI encoding (default @code{26}); overrides the preset.
@item cmacs-transcode-qp
Optional QP for GPU encoding.
@item cmacs-transcode-hwaccel
@code{off} (default), @code{auto}, or @code{force}.
@item cmacs-transcode-prefer-vulkan
Prefer the Vulkan path over VAAPI.
@item cmacs-transcode-prefer-software
Force software even when a device exists.
@item cmacs-transcode-auto-quality
Pick CRF/QP from the input width.
@item cmacs-transcode-resolution
Output scaling, e.g.@: @code{"1280x720"}.
@item cmacs-transcode-bitrate
Target bitrate, overriding CRF/QP.
@item cmacs-transcode-preset
ffmpeg encoder preset (ultrafast @dots{} veryslow).
@item cmacs-transcode-subtitles
Copy subtitle streams into mkv/mp4 (default @code{t}).
@item cmacs-transcode-preserve-color-metadata
Probe and re-emit colour metadata to avoid tint shifts (default @code{t}).
@item cmacs-transcode-color-primaries
@itemx cmacs-transcode-color-trc
@itemx cmacs-transcode-colorspace
@itemx cmacs-transcode-color-range
Explicit colour overrides (each @code{nil} = auto-detect).  Setting any one
disables ffprobe detection.
@end vtable

@subsection Audio

@vtable @code
@item cmacs-transcode-audio-format
Output format (default @code{"mp3"}).
@item cmacs-transcode-audio-quality
Named quality preset (default @code{"veryhigh"}).  The default @code{veryhigh}
is the maximum quality each lossy format supports: MP3 320@dmn{kbps},
Vorbis/OGG @option{-q:a 10} (~500@dmn{kbps}), Opus 256@dmn{kbps}, AAC
320@dmn{kbps}.
@item cmacs-transcode-audio-bitrate
Custom bitrate, e.g.@: @code{"320k"}, overriding the preset.
@item cmacs-transcode-audio-input-format
Input extension filter for directory adds, e.g.@: @code{"flac"}.
@end vtable

@code{cmacs-transcode-recursive} (default @code{nil}) enables recursive
directory-mirroring for both video and audio sessions (@pxref{Transcode
Recursive}).  The old audio-only @code{cmacs-transcode-audio-recursive} is
obsolete but still honoured for audio when set.

@subsection Tunable tables

The codec maps, quality/CRF presets, Vulkan bitrate presets, the per-format
audio bitrate/quality maps (@code{cmacs-transcode-mp3-presets},
@code{cmacs-transcode-vorbis-presets}, @code{cmacs-transcode-opus-presets},
@code{cmacs-transcode-aac-presets}), and the recognised input-extension lists
are all @code{defcustom}s too (@code{cmacs-transcode-video-codec-map},
@code{cmacs-transcode-crf-presets}, @code{cmacs-transcode-video-extensions},
@dots{}), so you can add codecs or retune presets without touching the code.

@node Transcode ffmpeg Mapping
@section What Options Produce

The argument builders are pure functions that mirror the scripts'
@code{build_ffmpeg_args}.  With the shipped video defaults, an input
@file{a.mp4} to @file{output/a.mkv} yields:

@example
ffmpeg -y -i a.mp4 -c:v libx265 -crf 26 \
       -c:a libvorbis -q:a 4 \
       -map 0:v -map 0:a? -map 0:s? -c:s copy \
       output/a.mkv
@end example

With @code{cmacs-transcode-preserve-color-metadata} on, ffprobe-detected
primaries/transfer/matrix/range are re-emitted (and, for software H.265, also
folded into @option{-x265-params}).  A VAAPI job substitutes
@option{-vaapi_device}, @code{hevc_vaapi}/@code{h264_vaapi}, and a
@code{format=nv12|vaapi,hwupload} filter; a Vulkan job uses
@code{hevc_vulkan}/@code{h264_vulkan} with a bitrate from the quality preset.
An audio job to MP3 produces:

@example
ffmpeg -y -i a.wav -c:a libmp3lame -b:a 320k \
       -vn -map 0:a -map_metadata 0 output/a.mp3
@end example

OGG/Vorbis uses the @option{-q:a} quality scale (@code{veryhigh} @result{}
@option{-q:a 10}); Opus and AAC use a @option{-b:a} bitrate from their preset
tables (@code{veryhigh} @result{} @code{256k} / @code{320k}); @code{copy}
stream-copies the audio.  With the shipped @code{veryhigh} default, every lossy
format is encoded at the maximum quality it supports.

@node Transcode Examples
@section Examples

@table @asis
@item Compress every mp4/mkv in a directory
Open Dired, mark the files, @kbd{M-x cmacs-transcode-video}, @kbd{RET}.  This is
the native equivalent of @command{compress_video --no-hwaccel --crf 26
./*.@{mp4,mkv@}}.
@item Two at a time
In the buffer press @kbd{P} @kbd{2} (or @kbd{p} to toggle to the configured
count), then @kbd{RET}.
@item Only fill in what is missing
Press @kbd{m} to enable the process-missing filter; already-transcoded outputs
are marked skipped.
@item Whole music library to OGG
@kbd{M-x cmacs-transcode-audio} on the library directory, press @kbd{R} to
enable recursion, @kbd{f} and choose @code{ogg}, then @kbd{RET}; the album
folder tree is mirrored under @file{output/} and each OGG is encoded at Vorbis
@option{-q:a 10} (the format's maximum, the shipped default).
@item Hardware-accelerated H.265
Press @kbd{H} to cycle to @code{auto} (VAAPI) or @code{vulkan}, then @kbd{RET}.
@end table
